pub const ANALYSIS: [&str; 2] = [
    "The comparison: the four matrices side by side, by what they did to one signal (its mean, spread and length after 1, 10 and 100 mixes) and by what they are (singular values, gain, distance from doubly stochastic and from orthogonal).",
    "How to read it, against what was written before the run: the doubly stochastic matrix's largest singular value is 1, its mean did not change and its spread fell at every count (the two channels the Birkhoff-geometry paper names: the mean kept exactly, the differences contracted), and the row error of Sinkhorn's iteration fell at every sweep: none of that counts against the constraint doing what its definition says. The free matrix's signal grew at a measured rate a little under its largest singular value, the scaled one's died away; and the free positive matrix also lost the original differences, the signal turned toward one direction. The orthogonal matrix kept the length at every count, and the spread stayed large though it changed, as did the mean, at the price of negative entries and a gain above 1 (a row's entries can add up past 1 in size even when no length grows). Which a network wants is not settled by one matrix; part 2, below, measures it in a stack.",
];

